<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Komancza Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Komancza</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny Ukrainian-oriented republic of Lemko villages in the Carpathians, which lasted from November 1918 to January 1919.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When Austro-Hungarian rule collapsed in November 1918, Ukrainian-minded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemkos</a> in about thirty villages around Komancza, in the eastern Lemko region of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a>, set up their own commissariat. Unlike the pro-Russian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lemko-rusyn-republic">Lemko-Rusyn Republic</a> further west, they aimed to join the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was a local self-government led by a council and the Greek Catholic priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pantaleimon-shpylka">Pantaleimon Shpylka</a>, with a small militia to keep order and guard against Polish units. It had little territory and no resources of its own, and it existed mainly to hold the area until it could be united with the Ukrainian state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In January 1919 Polish troops entered the area during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-ukrainian-war">Polish-Ukrainian War</a> and dissolved the republic. Its villages became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>, and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemko</a> inhabitants were deported in 1947.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/komancza-republic.jpg" alt="Komancza Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Ιγκόρ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ukraine_(1917%E2%80%931921).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Komancza</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pantaleimon Shpylka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rusyn, Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Lemko Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=komancza-republic" aria-label="Komancza Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5188 458 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Komancza Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5508 714L5551 699M5508 714L5473 630"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5551 699h0M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5508 714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5508 714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Komancza. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=komancza-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

